**Seed robustness:** 5 of 5 seeds have a lower weekday mean than Webster; the day-level difference is significant (raw p < 0.05) for only 2 of 5 seeds (2, 4). Days on which at least one seed is not better than Webster: 2026-09-21, 2026-09-23, 2026-09-24. 0 of 5 seeds beat Webster on every test weekday. The worst seed on test is seed 0 (validation rank 4 of 5); validation did not single it out (validation uses one replication per day and the seeds differ there by only 0.51 s/veh), so seed selection is not a reliable guard against an unstable seed.

Runs with unserved vehicles, teleports or collisions: 22 — real_nema 2026-09-18 r0 (1 teleports, 1 collisions, 0 unserved); real_nema 2026-09-23 r3 (1 teleports, 1 collisions, 0 unserved); real_nema 2026-09-24 r8 (483 teleports, 0 collisions, 0 unserved); real_nema 2026-09-28 r6 (1 teleports, 1 collisions, 0 unserved); rl_s0 2026-09-12 r0 (1 teleports, 0 collisions, 0 unserved); rl_s0 2026-09-24 r1 (1 teleports, 0 collisions, 0 unserved); rl_s0 2026-09-24 r2 (1 teleports, 0 collisions, 0 unserved); rl_s0 2026-09-24 r5 (1 teleports, 0 collisions, 0 unserved); rl_s0 2026-09-28 r1 (1 teleports, 0 collisions, 0 unserved); rl_s1 2026-09-24 r1 (1 teleports, 0 collisions, 0 unserved); rl_s2 2026-09-24 r1 (1 teleports, 0 collisions, 0 unserved); rl_s2 2026-09-24 r3 (1 teleports, 0 collisions, 0 unserved); rl_s2 2026-09-24 r4 (1 teleports, 0 collisions, 0 unserved); rl_s2 2026-09-24 r5 (1 teleports, 0 collisions, 0 unserved); rl_s2 2026-09-24 r8 (1 teleports, 0 collisions, 0 unserved); rl_s3 2026-09-24 r1 (1 teleports, 0 collisions, 0 unserved); rl_s3 2026-09-24 r4 (1 teleports, 0 collisions, 0 unserved); rl_s3 2026-09-24 r6 (1 teleports, 0 collisions, 0 unserved); rl_s4 2026-09-08 r0 (1 teleports, 0 collisions, 0 unserved); rl_s4 2026-09-24 r4 (1 teleports, 0 collisions, 0 unserved); rl_s4 2026-09-24 r8 (1 teleports, 0 collisions, 0 unserved); webster 2026-09-24 r7 (1 teleports, 0 collisions, 0 unserved)

## Caveats (apply to every number above)

- **Network (7065osm):** built from OpenStreetMap by tools/osm_build.py with documented tag fixes (intersections/7065osm/network/osm_tag_fixes.json): the Piedmont north leg (ways 525767115, 964301947, 1377947443) had no lanes/maxspeed tags, so netconvert defaulted to 1 lane and 100 km/h; set to the stop-line layout from GDOT's detectors (left, 2 through, right), 2 lanes upstream (matching the tagged neighbour) and 35 mph. Before the upstream fix a 1-lane stretch ~130 m upstream throttled this approach.
- **7065osm lane differences from the twin:** Piedmont from S has 2 left lanes (OSM tags 1 left + 3 through, but only 2 outbound lanes exist, so netconvert turned the 3rd through into a left; low left demand) and the right turn shares the outer through lane; the Piedmont-N left lane runs the whole 128 m segment (real bay is short), overstating left-turn storage.
- The twin's 1,920 veh/h/lane discharge figure in the capacity check was measured on the twin; an independent cyclic test gave ~2,000 on this network's Piedmont-N approach, with the queue reaching upstream.
- **Speeds:** the Piedmont-S approach and the Piedmont-S exit (carrying the Piedmont-N through movement) are OSM-tagged 40 mph (edges 1014001449#5/#9, 715642217#0, 715642215#0); the posted speed is 35 mph. Left as tagged; small effect (Piedmont-S delays 1–4 s lower than on the twin, partly also from its second left lane).
- Real timing on this network spills more on the highest-demand day than on the twin (Sep 24: 308 vs 173 s/veh, reaching movements that don't use Piedmont-N); cause not traced. 3 real-timing runs have one collision each (twin: none); not traced either. Neither affects the conclusions (weekday real timing is excluded as evidence on both networks).
- Single-intersection twin: no corridor coordination or platoon arrivals (Poisson within each 5-min count), which under-credits GDOT's coordinated Peachtree timing.
- Demand = GDOT's counted 5-min volumes (stop-bar detector counts); right turns are not counted.
- Twin capacity: driver imperfection sigma = 0.2 (SUMO default 0.5), so a through lane discharges about 1,920-2,010 veh/h of green in a cyclic queue-discharge test, at or slightly above the HCM base saturation flow (1,900) and above typical adjusted field values (~1,700-1,800) -- slightly optimistic. It is a standard-value calibration, not a fit to measured discharge at this intersection (intersections/7065/timing/capacity_check/README.md). Some PM count bins on Sep 21/23/24 exceed any physical discharge and are likely repeated pulses from set-back count detectors in stop-and-go queues; they are kept as recorded (fidelity policy).
- Webster is one all-day plan designed for the PM peak (no time-of-day plans); it can be beaten off-peak by any adaptive controller.
- RL: idealised detection (whole-approach lane occupancy, route-based left-turn calls); skips uncalled lefts (GDOT's actuated controller can too; Webster cannot).
- Real timing (actuated): SUMO's NEMA controller runs P1 before P2 (GDOT runs it after); the fixed exact-order row differs in both order and actuation, so the gap between the two rows bounds their combined effect. Its gap-out settings were fitted to Oct 2 records — a test day — which favours real timing on that day.
- Hardware parameters for all controllers come from GDOT's records, partly from a test day: minimum greens (RL, Webster, real) from the Oct 2 Timing & Actuation records; pedestrian walk/clearance times from the Oct 2 16:00 charts (cross-checked against all recorded windows).
- Saturdays: not chronologically held out (they fall inside the training/validation calendar span, but none was used for training or selection); Webster is the weekday-PM design applied unchanged; GDOT's weekend plans F/G/H run with gap-out settings fitted on a weekday and have no Timing & Actuation record of their own.
- Lane configuration not yet verified against the SR-237 widening construction state (see CLAUDE.md).
- All runs use SUMO 1.27.1, pinned in the project's uv environment (pyproject.toml) regardless of the system install; matrix.py refuses to run on any other version. An earlier run (archived, not used here) was on an OS-upgraded SUMO 1.28.0, which segfaulted intermittently; 1.27.1 has hit the same transient segfault at least once (matrix.py retries a crashed run up to 3x, which is deterministic and verified to reproduce), so the retry logic -- not the version alone -- is what makes these runs reliable.
- On the hand-built twin, the size of RL's advantage over Webster depends on this calibration; its direction does not. With sigma 0.5 (archive/v3_2026-10-15_sumo1.27.1_sigma0.5) RL beat Webster by 9.1 s/veh on weekdays and 6.1 on Saturdays; with sigma 0.2 the margin is smaller (see above) -- mostly because Webster's Peachtree-SW left was near breakdown at the lower capacity.
- Pedestrians are not simulated; Webster and RL respect pedestrian minimum greens every cycle.
- SUMO version: Eclipse SUMO sumo 1.27.1.

